  "id": "V045",
  "description": "Rule 3 stars: with Copeland and approval both tied in the runoff, the finalist with the most attendee stars wins",
  "spec_clause": "Phase 2 / Tally: 'Resolve in order: 1. Copeland score - most pairwise victories wins. 2. Approval - the higher-approved finalist wins (the approval fraction, not the raw yes count). 3. Stars - more star_votes among attendees wins. Below approval and not above it: a star may separate finalists that both the pairwise vote and standing approval have tied, and never promotes a film past a better-approved one. 4. Rotation fairness. 5. Shortest runtime. 6. Seeded random.'",
  "rationale": "5 attendees, 3 finalists, N_FINALISTS 5 so all three are promoted and no boundary tie is asserted. Every film holds all 5 rows with 4 yes and 1 no -> coverage 1.00, yes 4, approval 4/5 = 0.80 for each: identical on every rung above the stars. Stars, counted from attendees whose standing vote is a STARRED yes: m1 0, m2 2 (u1, u2), m3 1 (u1). A star is an upgraded yes, so it changes no yes-count and no approval. Round robin: m1 vs m2 = 3 (u1,u2,u3) to 2 -> m1; m2 vs m3 = 3 (u1,u2,u3) to 2 -> m2; m1 vs m3 = 2 (u1,u2) to 3 -> m3. Perfect cycle m1 > m2 > m3 > m1: no Condorcet winner, Copeland 1/1/1 -> rule 1 separates nothing; approval 0.80 across the board -> rule 2 separates nothing; rule 3 stars: m2's 2 > m3's 1 > m1's 0 -> m2 wins, decided_by 'stars'. This is the vector's whole point, and it is a discriminator in both directions: an implementation whose runoff chain has no star rung falls through to rule 4 rotation fairness, where u1 (m1) has never won and so has the earliest waiting_since 2026-01-01 while u2 last won 2026-06-01 and u3 2026-05-01 -> it promotes m1 by rotation_fairness, a different winner AND a different rule. Runtimes are equal (100) so nothing below fairness can mask that.",
